  "query_preview": "..\nThursday: A deep low pressure system has begun sliding down the\ncoast of British Columbia toward the Inland Northwest. High\nlevel clouds can be seen on satellite spreading across\nWA, OR, and ID out ahead of the low. Cool, moist air pushing \nonshore west of the Cascades has tightened the cross- Cascade \npressure gradient, and we are seeing breezy southwesterly winds\nthrough the Cascade gaps and across the Inland NW today as a \nresult. Winds will continue gusting to between 20 and 35 mph \ninto this evening. Combined with the warm and dry air still \npresent over Eastern WA and ID, the gusty winds are bringing \ncritical fire weather concerns with a Red Flag Warning in effect\nacross much of the region through 9pm tonight. \n\nFriday through Sunday: A substantial pattern change is in store\nfor Friday as the aforementioned deep low rolls into the \nNorthwest and remains over the region through the weekend. With\nthis low will come much cooler temperatures in the 60s to low\n70s, rain showers, and periods of afternoon thunderstorms. \nChances for showers look best on Friday and Sunday with the \ninitial plume of moisture and leading cold front passing \nthrough on Friday, then additional moisture wrapping around the \nlow and back into the region on Sunday.\n\nMountain areas have the highest chances (80-100 percent) of \nseeing greater than 0.10 inches of rain on Friday, while \nlowland areas including Spokane, the Palouse, the Columbia \nBasin, and the Okanogan Valley have a 30-70 percent chance of \nseeing more than 0.10 inches of precip. \n\nWinds will be periodically gusty through the period,\nparticularly in the lee of the Cascades. Fortunately, cooler \ntemperatures and higher humidity values will keep fire weather\nconcerns at bay.\n\nMonday through Thursday: Ensembles keep a troughing pattern over\nthe Northwest with potential for additional shortwaves moving\naround the broader trough. Temperatures look to gradually trend\nwarmer, staying in the 60s to 70s for the most part through at\nleast the middle of next week. Precipitation chances do not \nlook as robust heading into next week, but periodic chances for\nshowers will still be present over the mountains.",
